.audit-functions-baseline.json

# Working notes at the repository root — analyses, plans, critiques, briefings,
# orchestration prompts.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A SAFETY PATTERN, NOT TIDINESS. The source in this repository is
# public; the planning documents that accumulate in its root are not. One that is
# untracked but NOT ignored is a single `git add -A` away from a public commit.
# That has already happened twice in spert-forecaster, caught both times only
# because someone read the file list before pushing. A near-miss caught twice by
# attention is a hazard, not a track record.
#
# Measured 2026-08-11, before this block existed: 38 such files sat in this
# repository's root and `git check-ignore` matched exactly 0 of them.
#
# ⚠️ DENY-BY-DEFAULT WITH AN ALLOWLIST — deliberately NOT a vocabulary of
# filename patterns such as `/*-CRITIQUE-*.md` or `/*-PLAN-V*.md`. Measured
# across the suite: a vocabulary tuned to one repo's naming caught 4 of another
# repo's 108 documents, because the repos name things differently
# (`NAME-CRITIQUE-v2.md` versus `CRITIQUE_name.md`). An allowlist is complete by
# construction; a vocabulary is incomplete by construction.
#
# ⚠️ A vocabulary is also SELF-FLATTERING ON A MAC, which is the part that would
# not have been noticed. APFS is case-insensitive, so `/*-PLAN-V*.md` matches
# `...-plan-v3_1.md` here and matches nothing in CI. The same block measured 16
# on macOS and 4 under `git config core.ignorecase false`. The inflation happens
# where the pattern is authored; the deflation happens where it runs.
#
# The two failure directions are not symmetrical in a public repository:
#
#   - A doc wrongly ignored is visible on GitHub and fixed by one commit. It is
#     also not silent: `git add CONTRIBUTING.md` on an ignored path exits 1 and
#     prints a hint. Only `git add -A` passes over it quietly.
#   - A planning doc wrongly committed to a public repo is not recoverable. It is
#     in the history, cloneable and indexable.
#
# ⚠️ `.gitignore` has no effect on already-tracked files, so `/*.md` does not
# untrack anything listed below and never could. The allowlist is here so that
# creating a NEW SECURITY.md or CONTRIBUTING.md is still a plain `git add`, and
# so a reader can see at a glance what this repository means to publish.
#
# Derive the allowlist, never recall it:   git ls-files ':(glob)*.md'
/*.md
!/ARCHITECTURE.md
!/README.md
# Conventional root docs, not present today; listed so adding one needs no edit here.
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/SECURITY.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
# Root PDFs are renderings of the planning documents above (each has a sibling
# .md of the same name) — not site assets. Everything this site actually serves
# lives in public/, which this root-scoped rule does not touch.
/*.pdf

# Working scripts in scripts/ — the same safety pattern as the block above, one
# directory deeper.
#
# ⚠️ THE BLOCK ABOVE DOES NOT REACH HERE. `/*.md` and `/*.pdf` are root-scoped by
# design, so scripts/ was never covered. Measured 2026-08-11, before this block
# existed: four untracked working scripts sat in scripts/ and `git check-ignore`
# matched exactly 0 of them. Their content was not this repository's to publish,
# and in a public repository that is one `git add -A` from being unrecoverable.
# The hazard is not hypothetical here — it is the same one the root block was
# written for, found again in the next directory down.
#
# ⚠️ DENY-BY-DEFAULT WITH AN ALLOWLIST, for the reason argued at length above and
# not repeated here. A vocabulary rule — `scripts/*.py`, say — is tuned to the
# extension that happened to turn up, and catches nothing the next time the file
# is a .sh, an .rb or an .mjs. This shape is complete by construction.
#
# scripts/shipgate.mjs is tracked, and .gitignore has no effect on tracked files,
# so the negation below changes nothing today. It is here so that `git add` on it
# keeps working if it is ever untracked, and so a reader can see at a glance what
# this directory means to publish.
#
# Derive the allowlist, never recall it:   git ls-files scripts/
/scripts/*
!/scripts/shipgate.mjs
# run-rules-tests.mjs launches the Firestore rules suite and IS run by CI,
# so it has to be published. Note the failure mode if it is not: the gate
# passes locally, where the file exists, and dies in CI with MODULE_NOT_FOUND.
!/scripts/run-rules-tests.mjs
# check-deployed-rules.mjs is run by the drift workflow and must be published
# for the same reason — see the note above.
!/scripts/check-deployed-rules.mjs
# release-notes.mjs is run by the release workflow — same reason.
!/scripts/release-notes.mjs
# check-scheduler-mirror.mjs is run by scheduler-mirror-drift.yml — same reason.
!/scripts/check-scheduler-mirror.mjs
# check-pointers.mjs is `npm run check:pointers`, a pre-flight tool. It must be
# tracked for the same reason as the rest — and the negation has to come FIRST,
# before the file exists: while ignored it is invisible to
# src/guards/copyright-headers.test.ts, which enumerates
# `git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard` and so cannot see an
# IGNORED file. The header guard would then pass vacuously rather than earned.
!/scripts/check-pointers.mjs

# Firestore emulator output (npm run test:rules)
firestore-debug.log
firebase-debug.log
.firebase/
